WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.293
A display that

00:00:01.105 --> 00:00:02.130
holds its image

00:00:01.942 --> 00:00:03.510
for months unplugged

00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.700
Researchers in Australia

00:00:05.524 --> 00:00:06.620
built a reflective

00:00:06.432 --> 00:00:07.894
display from antimony

00:00:07.705 --> 00:00:09.155
selenide that keeps

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:09.792
its image for

00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:10.771
months without any

00:00:10.583 --> 00:00:11.290
power

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.304
Ordinary screens need

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:14.318
constant power to

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.037
hold a picture

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:16.440
but this phase-change

00:00:16.252 --> 00:00:17.796
material is rearranged

00:00:17.608 --> 00:00:18.516
at the atomic

00:00:18.328 --> 00:00:19.436
level and stays

00:00:19.247 --> 00:00:19.813
put

00:00:20.342 --> 00:00:21.438
Erasing it means

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.134
heating the film

00:00:21.946 --> 00:00:23.785
to around 200 degrees

00:00:23.597 --> 00:00:25.058
Celsius uniformly

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:26.226
letting the atoms

00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:27.323
move and find

00:00:27.134 --> 00:00:28.148
new positions

00:00:28.686 --> 00:00:29.700
Full story on

00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:30.407
the dashboard
